WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:10.400
On the 21st of March 1152, four French archbishops&nbsp;
gathered to annul the marriage of King Louis VII&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:19.040
of France and Eleanor of Aquitaine. The union&nbsp;
had failed to produce a male heir in a country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.280
where female succession to the throne&nbsp;
was prohibited. Only eight weeks later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:34.320
Eleanor married the man who became King Henry II&nbsp;
of England in 1154. Over the next half a century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:42.560
she reigned as queen consort and lived to see&nbsp;
two of her sons become King of England. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:49.440
role did Eleanor play in this tumultuous&nbsp;
era in the history of France and England?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:01:10.640
This is the story of Eleanor of Aquitaine. 
The woman known to history as Eleanor of Aquitaine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:17.920
was most likely born in 1122. There is&nbsp;
uncertainty around where she was born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:24.560
but it may have been in the western French city&nbsp;
of Poitiers. Eleanor’s father was William X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:31.600
Duke of Aquitaine and Gascony and Count of&nbsp;
Ponthieu. He is known as William the Saint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:39.520
owing to his untimely death whilst on pilgrimage&nbsp;
to Santiago De Compostela in Spain in 1137. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.440
premature passing set off a chain of events which&nbsp;
would catapult his daughter onto the centre stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:52.400
of European politics in the middle of the twelfth&nbsp;
century. Eleanor was originally named Alienor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:58.080
after her mother, Aénor de Châtellerault,&nbsp;
from the Latin meaning ‘the other Aenor’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:04.720
but her name became adulterated to Eleanor.&nbsp;
Her mother and father had married in 1121.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:11.040
Eleanor was their first child. A second daughter,&nbsp;
Petronilla, followed a couple of years after her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:18.480
and then a boy named William, who sadly died&nbsp;
when he was around four years old in 1130. 
	
&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.520
Eleanor’s father came from a cultured family&nbsp;
and she was taught history, literature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:32.880
astronomy and theology growing up, as well as more&nbsp;
female courtly pursuits like dancing, needle work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.200
and how to play musical instruments such as&nbsp;
the harp. Despite possessing these qualities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.960
Eleanor’s life, as was entirely common for&nbsp;
a daughter of a prominent medieval nobleman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.400
was destined to be shaped by marriage and&nbsp;
motherhood. Her position as her father’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.800
eldest daughter became especially important&nbsp;
after the death of her young brother in 1130,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.960
a tragedy that was compounded by the death&nbsp;
of Eleanor’s mother. What little chance that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.800
her father might remarry and have another male&nbsp;
heir was terminated when he died on pilgrimage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:15.840
in 1137. With this, Eleanor was placed under&nbsp;
the protection of King Louis VI of France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.960
who acted as her guardian in her teenage years.&nbsp;
Eleanor had now become an extremely important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:29.120
person in France’s politics, for the Duchy of&nbsp;
Aquitaine was a large territory that controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:36.080
much of the west of the country. Whoever married&nbsp;
her would acquire control over it. 
	
&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.080
Although Louis VI was one of Europe’s most&nbsp;
powerful monarchs, the French crown was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:50.240
nearly as powerful in the twelfth century as&nbsp;
it would become in early modern times, largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.240
because of the sheer power and autonomy of the&nbsp;
various dukedoms of the kingdom, such as Burgundy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:02.400
Normandy and Aquitaine, that rivalled the power&nbsp;
of the French crown. The roots of Aquitaine as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:09.680
distinct region of France date back to the time of&nbsp;
the Roman Empire, when it was known as Aquitania&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:16.160
Prima. It would later be ruled by the Visigoths&nbsp;
and then the Franks, two Germanic tribes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:22.240
had invaded the Western Roman Empire and carved&nbsp;
out their own principalities here in the fifth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:29.680
century AD. Eleanor’s ancestors were granted the&nbsp;
title of Dukes of Aquitaine in medieval times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.480
although it remained a fiefdom, meaning&nbsp;
that Eleanor’s family held the duchy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:40.800
the kings of France as their liege lords to&nbsp;
whom they owed homage. The Duchy of Aquitaine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:47.040
at its greatest extent, consisted of the&nbsp;
south-western quarter of modern-day France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:54.000
making it the largest and arguably the richest&nbsp;
of the French dukedoms. Whoever controlled it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:01.360
exercised considerable influence over France’s&nbsp;
politics. It was this colossal inheritance that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:08.960
Eleanor acquired in 1137. King Louis VI, who now&nbsp;
had Eleanor under his protection, consequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:16.560
devised a plan to marry the young heiress to his&nbsp;
son, Louis the Younger, the future King Louis VII.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.680
The only issue from the French king’s&nbsp;
perspective was a condition in Eleanor’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:29.440
father’s will that she should retain control over&nbsp;
the duchy until her children came of age. 
&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.040
Prince Louis had not always been the heir to&nbsp;
the French throne, as his older brother Philip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:44.400
had died in 1131, when his horse tripped over a&nbsp;
black pig that darted out of a dung heap in Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.520
resulting in the 15-year old prince being&nbsp;
thrown violently over the horse’s head and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:56.560
onto the ground. He was fatally injured and&nbsp;
died a day later. As the second-born son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:02.560
Louis had been preparing to embark on a pious&nbsp;
life of religion. The young prince’s religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:09.600
upbringing would go on to shape his character&nbsp;
and actions as king. Louis VI was anxious to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.120
make his new heir co-regent quickly after&nbsp;
the death of Philip as his own health was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:22.320
not good. This was linked to his obesity, and&nbsp;
Louis was known as Louis the Fat. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:29.600
in 1137, the king’s declining health was further&nbsp;
compounded by him contracting dysentery. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:36.160
prompted him to dispatch his son to Aquitaine,&nbsp;
along with 500 heavily armed knights. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:43.760
on the 25th of July 1137, Eleanor married young&nbsp;
Louis in the Cathedral of Saint-André in Bordeaux.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:50.800
Only a week later, Eleanor’s new husband became&nbsp;
King Louis VII of France after his father died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:57.360
It was customary for couples from noble families&nbsp;
at the time to exchange wedding gifts to further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:04.240
seal their union. Eleanor honoured this custom by&nbsp;
giving her new husband a rock-crystal vase that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:11.280
she had been given by her grandfather, William IX.&nbsp;
The artefact is still in existence today and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:17.680
kept in the Louvre Museum in Paris. It is widely&nbsp;
considered to be the only surviving object that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:26.240
we know for certain belonged to Eleanor.
After their marriage, Louis and Eleanor travelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:32.640
to Poitiers, the capital of Aquitaine, where they&nbsp;
were confirmed as Duke and Duchess of Aquitaine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.480
The festivities were short lived, as word soon&nbsp;
reached the couple that Louis’ father had died and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:44.640
he was now the King of France. They immediately&nbsp;
rode to Paris, where they were crowned king and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:52.720
queen consort on Christmas Day 1137. Eventually,&nbsp;
after this frenetic series of events, they settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.720 --> 00:08:00.400
down into married life. The young king, according&nbsp;
to contemporary accounts, was devoted to Eleanor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:06.880
but she quickly gained a reputation for acting in&nbsp;
an improper manner that was unbecoming of a woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:13.840
of her status. This hostility toward Eleanor was&nbsp;
largely due to the fact that she was an outsider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:19.440
unused to the customs of the Parisian&nbsp;
court. She was also, it must be remembered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:26.240
still a teenager of about 15 years. Whatever&nbsp;
the views of the wider court might have been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:32.160
Eleanor’s influence over her new husband became&nbsp;
clear one year after their wedding, when Louis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:38.080
along with a small army, put down a rebellion&nbsp;
near Poitiers to protect the Duchy of Aquitaine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.680
while three years later he undertook a campaign to&nbsp;
capture Toulouse, a city which Eleanor laid claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:52.000
to through her paternal grandmother, Phillipa. 
Although there is no evidence to suggest that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:57.760
Louis undertook these campaigns at his wife’s&nbsp;
insistence, it is certain that any campaign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:04.240
in Aquitaine or Toulouse would at the very least&nbsp;
have concerned her. What we also know for certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:10.800
is that Eleanor’s arrival at the French court&nbsp;
did cause some intrigue, as her younger sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:17.040
Petronilla, who had relocated to Paris with&nbsp;
Eleanor, began a relationship with the fifty-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:24.240
old cousin of the King, Raoul I of Vermandois. The&nbsp;
couple soon made a public declaration that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:30.320
intended to marry, despite the fact that Raoul&nbsp;
was already married to Eleanor of Champagne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:38.080
niece of Theobald II, Count of Champagne. In this&nbsp;
instance the queen once again used her influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:44.160
over the king to have Raoul and Eleanor’s marriage&nbsp;
annulled on the grounds that they were too closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:52.480
related. Petronilla and Raoul were then married&nbsp;
in 1142. Understandably this union greatly angered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:58.480
the Count of Champagne, who appealed to the head&nbsp;
of the Roman Catholic Church, Pope Innocent II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:04.480
who declared that Raoul’s marriage to Petronilla&nbsp;
was in turn invalid, meaning that both of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:10.880
now faced excommunication as a consequence. King&nbsp;
Louis, who had previously quarrelled with the Pope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:16.880
over the state of the Archbishopric of Bourges,&nbsp;
was in no mood to relent to Innocent’s decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:22.240
and so marched on Champagne with an army&nbsp;
and assaulted the capital of the lordship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:28.560
Vitry. It was burnt to ashes, while around&nbsp;
1,000 of the city’s inhabitants who had taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:36.160
refuge in a church also lost their lives. The war&nbsp;
against Champagne would go on for two more years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:43.200
until peace was finally agreed with the assistance&nbsp;
of the French abbot and prominent European cleric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:48.880
Bernard of Clairvaux. In a private meeting&nbsp;
with Eleanor he promised that God would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:55.600
grant her many children if she ceased to&nbsp;
be a negative influence over her husband.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:03.200
This had the desired effect, as both Eleanor and&nbsp;
her husband soon backed down, and Louis agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:10.400
to return Theobald’s captured lands to him. He&nbsp;
also agreed to cease his opposition to the Pope’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:18.560
choice for the Archbishopric of Bourges, although&nbsp;
Raoul and Petronilla remained excommunicated until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:26.800
Pope Innocent II’s death in 1144. 
For Eleanor the church’s blessing seemed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:32.640
have paid dividends. She gave birth&nbsp;
to a daughter named Marie in 1145,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:40.240
who would go on to marry the Count of Champagne’s&nbsp;
son, Henry, in 1164. The episode in Champagne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:46.720
demonstrates the influence she had acquired over&nbsp;
her husband, but even a monarch as powerful as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:53.680
Louis could not fight for long against his own&nbsp;
nobles and the Roman Catholic Church. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:59.360
Louis determined not long afterwards, with&nbsp;
the encouragement of the new Pope, Eugene III,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:06.800
to lead a Crusade against the Muslims in the Holy&nbsp;
Land. This Second Crusade was triggered by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:15.120
capture of the city of Edessa in the Holy Land by&nbsp;
Turkish Muslim warlord, Zengi, in December 1144.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:22.720
A year later, on Christmas Day 1145, in Bourges&nbsp;
Cathedral, the King made it known that he intended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:29.200
to take the Cross and set sail for the Middle&nbsp;
East. Eleanor also took the cross alongside her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:35.840
husband, the reasons for which are still debated&nbsp;
today. Some historians contend that Louis could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:41.040
not bear to leave his wife behind in France&nbsp;
and decided that she would travel with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:46.400
though others argue that it was Eleanor who&nbsp;
actually planted the idea of taking the cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:54.080
in her husband’s mind. She had a motive for doing&nbsp;
so. Her father’s brother, Raymond of Poitiers, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.080 --> 00:13:00.480
the Prince of Antioch, one of the four Christian&nbsp;
Crusader kingdoms that had been founded during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:06.480
First Crusade at the very end of the eleventh&nbsp;
century, along with the Kingdom of Jerusalem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:12.880
the County of Tripoli and the County of Edessa.&nbsp;
Eleanor’s participation in the Second Crusade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:19.840
would also ensure a large contingent of nobles and&nbsp;
soldiers from Aquitaine would join the king. 
&nbsp;

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:28.480
Whatever the motives were, the chronology&nbsp;
is clear. The king, his wife and their army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:37.440
of fellow crusaders set off from France in June&nbsp;
1147. They entered the Byzantine Empire in August,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:44.720
where they were received with some suspicion by&nbsp;
Emperor Manuel I Comnenus, who feared what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:51.360
influx of hordes of western Crusaders might bring&nbsp;
about in his dominions. Despite this hostility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:56.320
Eleanor made a considerable impression on&nbsp;
the Byzantines as they later portrayed her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:02.400
in accounts as a warrior queen who rode horses&nbsp;
in the male fashion at the head of the Crusader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:08.720
army. This interest in the French queen, was&nbsp;
reciprocated by the Crusader interest in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:15.600
Byzantines. They stayed for over a fortnight in&nbsp;
the city of Constantinople, the Byzantine capital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:22.480
Then they received a message that King Conrad III&nbsp;
of Germany, the other great European monarch who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:29.360
had become involved in the Second Crusade, had&nbsp;
secured a great victory against the Turks. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:35.840
wanting to miss out on the glory, Louis quickly&nbsp;
led his forces over the Bosporus and on towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:43.200
the Holy Land, However, upon entering Anatolia, he&nbsp;
learned that the message that had been received in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:49.280
Constantinople informing the French of Conrad’s&nbsp;
victory was false, and that the German King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:57.680
had actually been heavily defeated by the Seljuk&nbsp;
Turks at the Battle of Dorylaeum in October 1147.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:04.000
Judging that they had come too far to turn back,&nbsp;
the French Crusaders joined with Conrad’s depleted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:10.960
forces and pressed on into the hinterland of&nbsp;
Anatolia towards Antioch. Their journey would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:16.480
prove to be as difficult as it had been for&nbsp;
the Germans. Whilst approaching the summit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:24.160
Mount Cadmus on the 6th of January 1148, Louis&nbsp;
ordered his vanguard to halt and make camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:29.680
but its commanders, one of whom was&nbsp;
Eleanor’s vassal, Geoffrey de Rancon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:35.840
decided to continue into the next valley, which&nbsp;
stretched the Crusaders lines and made them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:42.560
vulnerable to attack. The Seljuks then launched a&nbsp;
surprise attack on their divided camps, resulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:48.320
in part of the Christian baggage train being&nbsp;
captured, while hundreds of Crusaders were slain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:55.520
as they retreated back down the narrow passes or&nbsp;
fell to their deaths in the deep ravines.
&nbsp;

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:02.720
Later Eleanor would be blamed by the chroniclers&nbsp;
for the defeat at the Battle of Mount Cadmus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:08.880
as it was suggested that she had given Rancon&nbsp;
the order for the crusader vanguard to march on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:14.480
splitting the Crusader forces in the process.&nbsp;
However, it should be noted that there is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:20.400
concrete evidence to suggest where Eleanor was&nbsp;
during this incident. Over the coming days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:25.440
the Turks mounted more raids on the crusaders,&nbsp;
as they traversed the Anatolian mountains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:31.440
and eventually Louis was forced to abandon many&nbsp;
of his men and proceed to the Holy Land by ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:38.000
which effectively consigned those left behind&nbsp;
to death from exposure, starvation or attack by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:45.440
the Seljuk Turks. The King and Eleanor finally&nbsp;
arrived at the city of Antioch in March 1148.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.880
There they were greeted by Eleanor’s uncle,&nbsp;
Raymond of Poitiers, but it soon became evident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:57.360
that there was a disagreement between the French&nbsp;
king and his wife. Louis was eager to proceed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:03.520
to Jerusalem, whilst Eleanor wanted the French to&nbsp;
remain at Antioch to aid Raymond in his planned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:09.760
siege of the city of Aleppo about 40 miles to&nbsp;
the east of Antioch. Eleanor, for her part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:15.120
spent many hours in private conversations&nbsp;
with her uncle for unknown reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:20.560
Some said she was seeking Raymond’s advice,&nbsp;
whilst others believed he was attempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:26.880
win her support for his plan to attack Aleppo,&nbsp;
whilst some chroniclers even accused Raymond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:33.040
and Eleanor of starting an incestuous&nbsp;
affair. Modern historians believe this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:39.920
to be false and the queen and Louis continued&nbsp;
to share a bed together after this period.
&nbsp;

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:48.000
It must be remembered that rumours of Eleanor’s&nbsp;
promiscuity abounded both during her life and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:54.640
following her death. The thirteenth-century&nbsp;
Minstrel of Rheims even claimed that Eleanor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.640 --> 00:18:01.280
had an affair with Saladin, the Muslim leader&nbsp;
during the Third Crusade forty years later. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:07.600
story is completely implausible, as Saladin was&nbsp;
a child living in Syria at the time that Eleanor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:12.880
was in the Holy Land. Whatever the purpose&nbsp;
of Eleanor’s conversations with her uncle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:18.960
they resulted in a disagreement over the Crusader&nbsp;
strategy, as Eleanor wished to remain in Antioch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:24.960
while Louis decided they would carry on to&nbsp;
Jerusalem. This was a clear sign of the growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:32.160
split between husband and wife. It was possibly at&nbsp;
this time that the idea was floated of a divorce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:38.400
based on consanguinity, the idea that the pair&nbsp;
were too closely related to have ever been legally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:45.280
able to marry. However, Eleanor and Louis were,&nbsp;
in reality, much more distantly related than many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:51.680
royal and aristocratic couples across medieval&nbsp;
Europe. In any event, it would be several years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:58.400
before the divorce was entered into. In the short&nbsp;
term, the Crusaders marched on to Jerusalem and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:05.520
entered the city in May 1148, where they were&nbsp;
welcomed by a jubilant crowd, which included the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:11.920
Patriarch of Jerusalem and Conrad. After finishing&nbsp;
his pilgrimage with prayer at Jerusalem’s holy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:19.040
sites, Louis embarked on a siege of the city of&nbsp;
Damascus. This enterprise ended after just four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:26.000
days on the 28th July in failure and retreat.&nbsp;
Moreover, the French resources were now running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:33.120
low, as was morale. There is very little mention&nbsp;
of Eleanor’s activities during this period. As was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:39.760
typical with Eleanor’s life, there arose numerous&nbsp;
legends about her activities in the Holy Land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:44.720
including one which suggested that she&nbsp;
brought back silkworms from the Middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:54.480
East and introduced them to Aquitaine. 
	
After celebrating Easter of 1149 in Jerusalem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.480 --> 00:20:01.200
Eleanor and Louis departed by ship towards Italy.&nbsp;
During this journey the royal ships were attacked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:06.800
and after narrowly escaping, the ships of Louis&nbsp;
and Eleanor were then separated by a violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:13.360
storm. Eleanor’s whereabouts remained unknown for&nbsp;
two months until her ship was finally brought to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:20.240
port at Palermo in Sicily. Where she was during&nbsp;
that two-month period remains a mystery to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:28.240
day. The king and queen were finally reunited in&nbsp;
Calabria in the late summer of 1149, but Eleanor’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:34.560
relief was soon dissipated by news that on the&nbsp;
29th of June, her uncle, Raymond of Antioch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:41.600
had been killed and decapitated at the Battle&nbsp;
of Inab in northern Syria by Muslim forces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:47.280
Greatly bereaved, Eleanor now set off with&nbsp;
her husband north towards Rome, where Pope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:55.200
Eugenius III had invited the royals to stay in his&nbsp;
palace at Tusculum just south of the city. Both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.200 --> 00:21:01.760
Louis and Eleanor separately confided in the Pope&nbsp;
about their marital problems during their visit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:07.680
but Eugenius adamantly refused to consider&nbsp;
an annulment, as they already had a child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:13.520
and had been married for many years. Instead,&nbsp;
he blessed the marriage and commanded that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:18.480
should not be dissolved under any pretext,&nbsp;
even encouraging the couple to sleep in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:27.760
the same bed when residing with him. 
On the 11th of November 1149, Louis and Eleanor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:34.240
at last returned to Paris after an absence of two&nbsp;
and a half years. Although they were received by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:40.160
demonstrations of joy, there was underlying&nbsp;
discontent pervading France at the complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:46.480
failure of the Second Crusade. The resentment&nbsp;
heightened the discontent between Eleanor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:52.720
her husband. Strangely, their already tense&nbsp;
marriage was irreparably broken by the birth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.720 --> 00:22:00.000
of their second child, a daughter named Alix, in&nbsp;
the late summer of 1150, as the arrival of another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.000
daughter was a bitter disappointment to Louis,&nbsp;
who, approaching the age of thirty, craved a male&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:12.800
heir to succeed him in the Capetian royal line.&nbsp;
Indeed, he took this birth of a second daughter as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:19.120
a sign that God did not approve of his marriage.&nbsp;
This view was shared by many of his advisors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:24.640
who encouraged Louis to divorce Eleanor and marry&nbsp;
someone less controversial who would deliver him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:31.520
a son. Louis agreed to initiate proceedings to&nbsp;
acquire an annulment in late 1151, and on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:38.400
21st of March 1152, four archbishops of France&nbsp;
gathered at the royal castle of Beaugency on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:44.640
Loire River to carry out the separation after&nbsp;
receiving the reluctant approval of the Pope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:50.960
This divorce had immense consequences not just&nbsp;
for Eleanor, but for the future of the Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:56.800
European balance of power, as by renouncing his&nbsp;
marriage, the King of France had also renounced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.800 --> 00:23:05.280
his claim to the Duchy of Aquitaine. 
Following her divorce, Eleanor became the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:11.680
coveted woman in Western Europe. Nevertheless,&nbsp;
Eleanor already knew who she was going to marry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:17.360
Indeed, it is likely she had planned it well&nbsp;
before her divorce from Louis, as only eight weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:23.760
after the annulment, Eleanor was again standing&nbsp;
at the altar, this time beside Henry of Anjou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:31.280
the future King Henry II of England. Eleanor had&nbsp;
first met Henry in Paris in 1151. Though she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:36.960
eleven years his senior, they were attracted&nbsp;
to one another and, perhaps more importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:43.360
both Eleanor and Henry stood to gain politically&nbsp;
from the marriage. At this time, Henry’s lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:49.200
included the northern French provinces of Anjou&nbsp;
and Normandy, while Henry, who was the grandson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:55.840
of King Henry I, had a strong claim to succeed&nbsp;
King Stephen of England and had been engaged in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.840 --> 00:24:03.120
a protracted war to try to cement his claim to&nbsp;
the English throne. After arriving at Poitiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:09.680
Eleanor sent envoys to Henry requesting marriage,&nbsp;
then summoned the chief vassals of her lands to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:16.640
renew their allegiance to her, and to approve&nbsp;
her choice of husband. On the 18th of May 1152,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:22.960
Eleanor and Henry were married in the cathedral&nbsp;
of Saint-Pierre at Poitiers, much to the fury of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:29.680
Louis VII, who viewed Henry as a rival. Now, with&nbsp;
the addition of Eleanor’s considerable provinces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:35.920
which more than doubled Henry’s holdings in&nbsp;
France, Louis was left vulnerable. What was worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:41.920
Eleanor’s second marriage soon resulted in the&nbsp;
sons that she and Louis had never been able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:48.800
have. Over the course of her marriage to Henry,&nbsp;
Eleanor bore eight children, including five sons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:56.880
William, Henry, Richard, Geoffrey and John.&nbsp;
The eldest, William, died at the age of three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.880 --> 00:25:02.560
but Richard, who would come to be known as the&nbsp;
‘Lionheart’, and John would both inherit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:09.440
English crown in later years. The three daughters&nbsp;
were named Matilda, Eleanor and Joan. They would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:14.240
go on to marry some of the most powerful&nbsp;
men in Europe, including Henry the Lion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:22.080
Duke of Saxony and Bavaria, Alfonso VIII, King&nbsp;
of Castile, and William II, King of Sicily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:30.640
Eleanor’s unofficial title as, The ‘First&nbsp;
Grandmother of Europe’ is well deserved. 
&nbsp;

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:37.840
In January 1153, after he and Eleanor had&nbsp;
completed a tour of Aquitaine, Henry set sail for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:44.240
England at the head of an invasion force, intent&nbsp;
on bringing King Stephen to submission. Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:50.080
was now determined to seize the English crown by&nbsp;
force, and after months of successful skirmishing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:55.600
during which Henry took many English towns and&nbsp;
castles and cemented a reputation for bravery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.600 --> 00:26:03.200
and military skill, many English barons and&nbsp;
bishops urged the two rivals to negotiate. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:11.040
the death of his eldest son, Eustace, on the 17th&nbsp;
of August, King Stephen agreed, and in a November&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:17.840
peace conference, he accepted Henry’s claim to the&nbsp;
throne and agreed to recognise him as his heir.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:24.800
In return, Henry conceded that Stephen would rule&nbsp;
England peacefully until his death. Henry did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:33.680
have long to wait, as on the 25th of October 1154&nbsp;
King Stephen died. Thus, Henry and Eleanor set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:39.200
sail for England, to take possession of their&nbsp;
new kingdom. They were crowned in Westminster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:46.960
Abbey on the 19th of December 1154, after being&nbsp;
received by a jubilant London crowd, who hailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:53.120
the new royal couple as peacemakers, come to&nbsp;
restore order after the years of the Anarchy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.120 --> 00:27:00.240
that had characterised the reign of King Stephen.&nbsp;
The new royal line established by Henry was known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:10.240
as the House of Plantagenet. Its members would&nbsp;
rule England for the next 330 years. 
	
&nbsp;

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:16.240
Though Henry rapidly established his authority&nbsp;
over England, implementing legal reforms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:22.160
consolidating his power, he found his continental&nbsp;
holdings much more difficult to govern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:30.000
particularly Aquitaine, whose nobles were hostile&nbsp;
to outsiders. Eleanor consequently had to exercise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:36.480
an unusual degree of influence over the duchy for&nbsp;
a queen consort. Furthermore, though Henry ensured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:41.760
he possessed complete control over the most&nbsp;
important matters of state, during the reign’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:48.480
early years, Eleanor acted as regent of England on&nbsp;
the numerous occasions when Henry was away on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:55.680
continent, running parts of the government in his&nbsp;
absence. As queen, and as a pious woman, Eleanor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:55.680 --> 00:28:02.080
was a great benefactor of religious institutions.&nbsp;
She funded the building and restoration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:08.560
churches, abbeys and cathedrals in Poitou and&nbsp;
Aquitaine, as well as the Abbey of Fontevrault&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:14.800
in the Loire Valley, which benefited most&nbsp;
from Eleanor’s patronage. Under her influence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:21.280
the religious Order of the Abbey grew in influence&nbsp;
and prestige. It was here that Eleanor would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:28.320
eventually spend her final years and where she was&nbsp;
buried alongside Henry and her son Richard. 
&nbsp;

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:37.600
In 1168, Eleanor took up residence in&nbsp;
Poitiers, establishing her court in the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:43.200
along with her favourite son Richard. Although&nbsp;
it was Eleanor that initiated this separation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:48.800
from Henry, the king approved of it and was&nbsp;
not upset or disturbed by the queen’s move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:54.800
as having dealt with a large Aquitanian revolt&nbsp;
in the spring of 1168, Henry thought Eleanor’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.800 --> 00:29:01.120
presence would help pacify the duchy. There is&nbsp;
much debate as to why Eleanor decided to spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:07.280
time at Poitiers. Perhaps her marriage had become&nbsp;
strained. Henry was frequently unfaithful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:13.360
Eleanor was in her mid-forties after bearing many&nbsp;
children. It is possible that Eleanor believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:18.960
she had done her duty within the marriage and&nbsp;
no longer wanted to take an active part in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:24.080
Furthermore, living in her own lands with a&nbsp;
relative degree of autonomy, allowed Eleanor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:31.200
to exercise greater power than when she was&nbsp;
with Henry in England. From 1168 to 1173,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:36.960
Eleanor maintained her own court at Poitiers,&nbsp;
one that has become legendary as the so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:42.960
‘Court of Love’, where chivalric romance was&nbsp;
prominent, and the medieval lyrical poetry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:48.720
of the troubadours flourished under Eleanor’s&nbsp;
patronage. The queen herself was often mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:54.800
in the poems of the troubadours of south-western&nbsp;
France at the time, described admiringly as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.800 --> 00:30:01.360
elegant and noble woman. However, like so many&nbsp;
of the stories surrounding the life of Eleanor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.360 --> 00:30:09.520
her ‘Court of Love’ was almost certainly a myth, a&nbsp;
literary conceit concocted by Andreas Capellanus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:16.800
a chaplain at the court of Troyes. He wrote&nbsp;
The Art of Courtly Love in the late 1180s which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:23.360
describes Eleanor, along with her daughter Marie,&nbsp;
as presiding over a tribunal that made judgements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:30.720
on intellectual disputes concerning courtly love.&nbsp;
Despite these writings, there is no real evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:36.240
for this ‘Court of Love’ in any contemporary&nbsp;
sources. Furthermore, there is no evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:43.360
that Marie ever visited Eleanor at Poitiers.&nbsp;
Thus, Eleanor’s appearance in Andreas’ work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:50.800
owes far more to her reputation than&nbsp;
to her actual actions. 
	
&nbsp;

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:56.720
Whilst Eleanor was at Poitiers, pressure was&nbsp;
growing on King Henry II’s rule from his eldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.720 --> 00:31:03.840
son Henry. With the treaty of Montmirial&nbsp;
in January 1169, the king divided his vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:11.360
inheritance between his three eldest sons. Henry&nbsp;
was to receive England, Normandy and Anjou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:17.520
Richard would be granted Aquitaine, while&nbsp;
Geoffrey would acquire Brittany. Furthermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:24.240
the young Henry was crowned as King of England in&nbsp;
June 1170, in anticipation of his succession, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:30.800
was dubbed the ‘Young King’. However, the Young&nbsp;
King took after his father in his restless desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:38.320
for power. He found having titles without any real&nbsp;
power infuriating, and in his anger a rift began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:44.960
to grow between the Young King and his father, who&nbsp;
refused to cede any effective political power to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:52.160
his son. Furthermore, King Henry was increasingly&nbsp;
isolated politically, primarily due to the murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:59.520
of the Archbishop of Canterbury, Thomas Becket,&nbsp;
in December 1170 by four of Henry’s knights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:06.080
an act which Henry had not explicitly ordered&nbsp;
and deeply regretted, yet one in which he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:12.160
still implicated, and which shocked Christian&nbsp;
Europe. Eleanor was amongst those who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:18.880
angry at Henry for the incident, and after living&nbsp;
separately for five years she sided firmly on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:25.440
side of her sons in the growing family quarrels.&nbsp;
She knew that the more power that they gained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:31.440
the more political influence she herself&nbsp;
would hold in western France. 
&nbsp;

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:39.040
Henry was also coming under growing pressure from&nbsp;
his vassals, especially in Poitou and Aquitaine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:45.120
who resented his heavy-handed imposition of&nbsp;
centralised royal authority. After Henry’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.120 --> 00:32:51.440
dramatic loss of international prestige following&nbsp;
the murder of Becket, they sensed an opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:56.800
to overthrow the authority of the English King,&nbsp;
and their growing rebelliousness was encouraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.800 --> 00:33:02.960
by Eleanor, her sons, and King Louis of France,&nbsp;
who relished the chance to undermine the might of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:10.400
Henry’s continental empire. The stage was thus set&nbsp;
for the most dangerous rebellion ever to confront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:16.720
Henry II. Although Eleanor’s precise role in&nbsp;
the build-up to the uprising is not certain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:22.640
it is clear that she was a key player in helping&nbsp;
to form the hostile coalition that confronted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:29.680
English King in 1173. In March of the same year,&nbsp;
the Young King escaped from his father and fled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:35.680
to Paris, where he and King Louis pledged to&nbsp;
assist each other against their common enemy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:41.680
Eleanor soon sent his younger brothers, Richard&nbsp;
and Geoffrey, to Paris, to join him in revolt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:47.680
whilst encouraging the lords of her southern lands&nbsp;
to rise up. Fearing for her safety in Poitiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:47.680 --> 00:33:53.840
Eleanor left for Paris too sometime between&nbsp;
the end of March and the beginning of May 1173.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:59.760
However, on her journey the queen was captured&nbsp;
by men in Henry’s pay and sent to his castle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:07.280
in Rouen. For the King, her actions constituted&nbsp;
an extremely bitter betrayal and he had Eleanor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.280 --> 00:34:14.320
confined as his prisoner. Contemporary chroniclers&nbsp;
were unanimous in condemning Eleanor’s treachery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:20.960
as her actions violated every medieval&nbsp;
concept of the duties and loyalties of a wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:27.040
Whilst sons rebelling against fathers was&nbsp;
a fairly common occurrence in this period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:34.800
it was virtually unheard of for a queen&nbsp;
to rebel against her husband. 
&nbsp;

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:43.360
The Rebellion of 1173 to 1174 saw little open&nbsp;
warfare. However, many castles were besieged and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:50.160
lands ravaged as Henry’s sons and the King of&nbsp;
France used scorched earth tactics to decimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.160 --> 00:34:56.480
swathes of the English king’s territory. Yet,&nbsp;
despite the strength of their coalition, the Young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.480 --> 00:35:04.000
King and Louis were unable to effectively organise&nbsp;
the divided rebel groups into a cohesive force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:09.600
while Henry II retained the support of&nbsp;
the vast majority of the English nobles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:18.880
who feared invasion and instability above all. The&nbsp;
summer of 1173 saw Henry fight tirelessly across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:26.080
his lands to suppress the rebellion. After a&nbsp;
winter lull, the spring of 1174 saw fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.080 --> 00:35:32.320
break out again in force. Then the rebels’ lack&nbsp;
of unity and Henry’s ceaseless energy saw the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:37.760
revolt begin to lose momentum after the invasion&nbsp;
of England by William the Lion, King of the Scots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:44.320
was repulsed. William was captured by Henry’s&nbsp;
troops led by Ranulf de Ganvill in the summer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:51.760
of 1174, leading to the English rebels suing&nbsp;
for peace that July. William even acknowledged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:57.520
Henry as his feudal overlord. This forced&nbsp;
Louis and the Young King to call off their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:57.520 --> 00:36:03.600
planned invasion of England. Then, when their&nbsp;
attack on Rouen in August ended in retreat back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:10.080
to Paris in the face of Henry’s advance, the&nbsp;
rebellion was virtually at an end. For Henry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:16.800
this was a great victory against considerable&nbsp;
odds. It restored his international reputation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:22.720
from the tarnished state it had been in&nbsp;
after the death of Saint Thomas Becket. 
&nbsp;

00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:31.680
The outcome of these events was that a peace was&nbsp;
reached on the 30th September 1174 between Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:38.400
and his sons at Montlouis. The King generously&nbsp;
gave money and castles to his remorseful sons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:45.920
but he did not cede any real political power.&nbsp;
Unusually, he inflicted a distinct revenge on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:52.400
Eleanor. While Henry proclaimed a general amnesty&nbsp;
for all those who had risen up against him, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:59.280
was not extended to his wife. After travelling&nbsp;
to England with Henry on the 8th of July 1174,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:05.920
Eleanor was placed under house arrest. For&nbsp;
the remainder of Henry’s life, until 1189,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.920 --> 00:37:12.160
she would remain a virtual prisoner and was only&nbsp;
permitted limited contact with her children. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:18.640
King dealt with Eleanor so discreetly that very&nbsp;
little is known about her life during these years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.640 --> 00:37:25.040
She was mostly kept under detention at Winchester&nbsp;
Castle. Although Eleanor lived in fairly luxurious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:25.040 --> 00:37:30.880
surroundings here, the king ensured she was&nbsp;
completely cut off from the outside world, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:37.280
he had first-hand proof of how dangerous Eleanor&nbsp;
could be. The king then enjoyed several years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.280 --> 00:37:44.560
peace before the Young King revolted again in&nbsp;
the early 1180s. Richard’s harsh suppression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.560 --> 00:37:51.040
of rebellious barons and the ruthless enforcement&nbsp;
of his authority in Aquitaine, which he now ruled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:57.760
in Eleanor’s absence, caused his bitter vassals&nbsp;
to look to the Young King to challenge Richard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:57.760 --> 00:38:03.520
And so the impetuous Young Henry joined with&nbsp;
the Barons of Aquitaine and invaded Richard’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:10.960
territory in 1182. When Henry II stopped his&nbsp;
eldest son’s allowance in response, the Young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:17.360
King formed a mercenary band and raided and looted&nbsp;
territories throughout southern France. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:17.360 --> 00:38:25.360
in June 1183, the Young King fell violently&nbsp;
ill with dysentery and died at the age of 28.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:30.960
After the shock death of the Young King, from&nbsp;
the late summer of 1183, Eleanor was allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:37.360
greater freedom, resuming her place as Queen and&nbsp;
occasionally appearing at Henry’s side in public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.360 --> 00:38:45.360
but there were no obvious signs that the&nbsp;
couple’s personal estrangement had ended. 
&nbsp;

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:52.800
By 1189, Henry had reached the age of 56.&nbsp;
Although he was twelve years younger than Eleanor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:59.040
a lifetime of relentless activity had left him&nbsp;
suffering from numerous health problems, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:06.240
a bleeding ulcer. Thus, only six years after the&nbsp;
death of Young Henry, the King died on the 6th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.240 --> 00:39:15.200
July 1189 in his castle at Chinon and was buried&nbsp;
at the nearby Fontevraud Abbey. And so, Richard I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:21.920
was crowned King of England in Westminster Abbey&nbsp;
on the 3rd September 1189, following his father’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:29.200
death, in front of an enthusiastic gathering. One&nbsp;
of his first acts as king was to formally order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:29.200 --> 00:39:35.120
the release of Queen Eleanor from captivity.&nbsp;
Despite having been a prisoner for 15 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:41.920
Eleanor emerged with ferocity onto the European&nbsp;
political scene at the age of 67. For the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:48.000
remaining 15 years of her life, she would play a&nbsp;
more influential political role than ever before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:54.160
as the queen was able to exert far more influence&nbsp;
over her children, than she did over her late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.160 --> 00:40:00.720
husband. With Richard in Aquitaine, Eleanor set&nbsp;
about drumming up support for her son in England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:06.560
a country that Richard had barely visited. Upon&nbsp;
travelling to Westminster, the Queen received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:12.320
oaths of loyalty from the lords of England on&nbsp;
behalf of the King. In addition, Eleanor passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:18.560
a host of laws, in an attempt to endear the&nbsp;
English people to their new king, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.560 --> 00:40:25.280
ordering the release of vassals who had rebelled&nbsp;
against Henry II. The 31-year-old Richard, sailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:25.280 --> 00:40:32.000
to Portsmouth on the 13th of August, where, thanks&nbsp;
largely to Eleanor’s efforts, he was greeted with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:39.040
great public enthusiasm. Nonetheless, Richard the&nbsp;
Lionheart, who was a French lord by temperament,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:39.040 --> 00:40:44.960
had very little interest in administering&nbsp;
his new domain. Rather he immediately threw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:50.720
himself into preparing for the project that&nbsp;
dominated his imagination – the Third Crusade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:50.720 --> 00:40:57.520
On the 2nd of October 1187 the Muslims led by&nbsp;
their brilliant military commander, Saladin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:57.520 --> 00:41:03.920
had occupied Jerusalem and massacred the Knights&nbsp;
Templar, causing the leaders of Western Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.920 --> 00:41:11.840
urged on by Popes Urban III and Clement III, to&nbsp;
initiate a new crusade. After implementing heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:17.600
taxes on the English people and the nobility&nbsp;
to pay for his supplies, Richard departed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:17.600 --> 00:41:24.800
from England in December 1189 for France with the&nbsp;
ultimate aim of making his way to the Holy Land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:31.200
In his absence he left Eleanor unofficially&nbsp;
in charge of the English government. 
&nbsp;

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:40.000
Her imprisonment had made Eleanor increasingly&nbsp;
pious, yet her tenacious energy had not been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:45.360
eroded in the slightest, and in Richard’s&nbsp;
absence she transacted the business of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:51.520
state using her own seal on official documents,&nbsp;
demonstrating the qualities of a benevolent ruler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:57.120
and greatly impressing contemporary chroniclers.&nbsp;
Eleanor also had to deal with the intrigues of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:57.120 --> 00:42:03.200
Richard’s younger brother, John, who was furious&nbsp;
at Richard’s intention to name his four-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:09.200
nephew, Arthur of Brittany, as his heir He&nbsp;
travelled up and down England in an attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:09.200 --> 00:42:16.880
to gain support amongst the English. Philip II of&nbsp;
France had left the Crusade due to illness. Upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:23.200
his return to France in 1191, Eleanor realised&nbsp;
that the King presented another threat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:23.200 --> 00:42:29.600
Richard’s authority in Europe, and after John and&nbsp;
Philip formed an alliance in early 1191, the Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:29.600 --> 00:42:36.000
spent considerable time and energy, thwarting the&nbsp;
intrigues of these two princes during Richard’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:42.560
absence. Upon hearing in February 1192 that John&nbsp;
was preparing to cross the channel with an army to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:42.560 --> 00:42:48.560
join with Philip, Eleanor travelled to England&nbsp;
from Normandy to stop him. After making every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.560 --> 00:42:54.080
English magnate swear a new oath of fealty&nbsp;
to Richard, Eleanor threatened to confiscate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.080 --> 00:43:00.800
John’s castles and estates if he defied her.&nbsp;
He backed down initially, but as the crusaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:07.920
began arriving home in the winter of 1192, having&nbsp;
failed to recapture Jerusalem, Eleanor received a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:07.920 --> 00:43:14.160
letter from the Holy Roman Emperor, Henry VI,&nbsp;
stating that Richard had been taken captive by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:21.200
the Emperor’s cousin, Duke Leopold of Austria,&nbsp;
whom he had offended whilst on crusade. 
	
&nbsp;

00:43:22.800 --> 00:43:29.200
Leopold’s overlord, Henry VI, was a ruthless&nbsp;
man, who was an enemy of the Plantagenets after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:36.880
Henry II had supported his great rival, Henry the&nbsp;
Lion, Duke of Saxony. Leopold now sought to exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:43.920
revenge on his son and heir. After taking control&nbsp;
of the prisoner, Henry demanded an extortionate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:52.240
ransom of 100,000 silver marks, a sum equivalent&nbsp;
to twice the annual revenue of England. With her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.240 --> 00:43:58.320
customary vigour, Eleanor set to work raising the&nbsp;
King’s ransom, whilst keeping the kingdom united&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.320 --> 00:44:04.160
and again blocking John’s attempts to claim the&nbsp;
throne. Though England was already financially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:10.880
exhausted after the crusade, Eleanor imposed a&nbsp;
levy demanding a quarter of every person’s annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:10.880 --> 00:44:16.800
income, as well as extracting wealth from churches&nbsp;
and abbeys; nor were these efforts limited to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:16.800 --> 00:44:23.120
England, as Eleanor sent officials into Anjou and&nbsp;
Aquitaine to collect ransom money. With the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:23.120 --> 00:44:30.320
raised, Eleanor herself set out for Germany in&nbsp;
December 1193, and was received on 2nd February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:37.840
1194 by the Emperor at his court in Mainz.&nbsp;
However, Henry VI then announced that Philip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.840 --> 00:44:44.720
and John had offered him an even greater ransom&nbsp;
sum, to hand over Richard to the King of France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.720 --> 00:44:50.720
and it was only after 48 hours of negotiations&nbsp;
that Eleanor and Richard’s German allies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.720 --> 00:44:56.160
persuaded the Emperor to release the King.&nbsp;
With the triumphant return of the crusader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:56.160 --> 00:45:03.040
king to England on the 12th of March 1194, all&nbsp;
rebellious support for John was crushed. Richard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:03.040 --> 00:45:08.960
quickly re-established his authority, forgiving&nbsp;
his troublesome younger brother, whom he did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:08.960 --> 00:45:18.240
regard as a serious threat. John was obedient to&nbsp;
his brother for the remainder of his reign. 
&nbsp;

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:23.280
The same could not be said of King Philip&nbsp;
of France. During the final five years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:28.480
Richard’s short reign, the English king was&nbsp;
engaged in a bitter struggle with Philip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.480 --> 00:45:34.080
tirelessly defending his territories against&nbsp;
the aggressive ambition of the French King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:38.720
Eleanor meanwhile, lived at the Abbey of&nbsp;
Fontevrault for the final years of Richard’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:47.120
reign, looking toward the salvation of her soul&nbsp;
and death, as by 1199 the Queen was 77 years old.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:53.520
Richard’s conflict with Philip was brought to an&nbsp;
end on the 13th of January 1199 when a five-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.520 --> 00:45:59.840
truce was concluded without the Lionheart ceding&nbsp;
any of his territory to Philip. Richard’s reign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:59.840 --> 00:46:06.080
would end less than three months later when he was&nbsp;
struck by an arrow whilst besieging the castle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:06.080 --> 00:46:14.160
a rebellious Aquitanian vassal. He died of his&nbsp;
wound on the 6th of April 1199 at the age of 41&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:21.040
after declaring John as his heir. Eleanor was at&nbsp;
the bedside of her beloved son when he perished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:26.800
and she saw to it that he was buried at the Abbey&nbsp;
of Fontevrault, at the foot of his father’s tomb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:33.680
Of immediate importance to Eleanor, following&nbsp;
Richard’s death, was to ensure that her son John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.680 --> 00:46:39.840
received his inheritance, as the ever troublesome&nbsp;
Philip II proclaimed Richard’s nephew Arthur of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.840 --> 00:46:45.680
Brittany, who was residing with the French King&nbsp;
in Paris, as the rightful heir to his empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.680 --> 00:46:52.160
leading to numerous vassals in the province of&nbsp;
Anjou declaring themselves for Arthur. The Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.160 --> 00:46:58.800
responded by ordering the devastation of the lands&nbsp;
of any vassal disloyal to her son, and Arthur’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:05.200
support in Anjou was soon quashed. Furthermore,&nbsp;
Eleanor embarked on a tour of her southern lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.200 --> 00:47:11.200
to consolidate support for John in Aquitaine and&nbsp;
Poitou. When John was crowned as King of England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.200 --> 00:47:21.360
in May 1199 his continental holdings were secure,&nbsp;
thanks largely to the actions of his mother. 
&nbsp;

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:28.560
In late 1199, Philip concluded a truce with&nbsp;
John, recognising the English King as Richard’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:33.840
successor and providing for the marriage of&nbsp;
Philip’s heir to one of John’s Castilian nieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:39.360
the daughters of his sister Eleanor. It was&nbsp;
decided that Queen Eleanor should travel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:39.360 --> 00:47:45.440
Castile to select one of the princesses and&nbsp;
convey them back to France, a strenuous journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.440 --> 00:47:51.680
for a woman in her late seventies, yet Eleanor&nbsp;
welcomed the opportunity to see her daughter for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:51.680 --> 00:47:58.720
the first time in 30 years. Following the truce,&nbsp;
Eleanor set off from Poitiers and was ambushed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.720 --> 00:48:05.920
captured just south of the city by a rebellious&nbsp;
vassal, Hugh de Lusignan, who demanded she grant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:05.920 --> 00:48:12.000
him the title of Count of La Marche in return for&nbsp;
her release. She agreed after deciding that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:17.760
Castilian marriage was of greater importance&nbsp;
than a disputed fiefdom. She then crossed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.760 --> 00:48:25.040
Pyrenees and travelled onwards to the court of&nbsp;
Castile in late January 1200. There she chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:25.040 --> 00:48:30.960
16-year-old Blanche to marry the French heir,&nbsp;
a choice that would prove to be a wise one, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:30.960 --> 00:48:37.680
Blanche of Castile would ultimately become almost&nbsp;
as formidable a queen as her grandmother, keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:44.800
France stable during the minority of her son, the&nbsp;
future Saint, Louis IX. Eleanor arrived back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.800 --> 00:48:51.520
southern France in April, where she soon made her&nbsp;
final impact on the European political stage after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:58.000
war again broke out between John and Philip. The&nbsp;
queen immediately declared her support for John,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:58.000 --> 00:49:04.000
outraged at Arthur of Brittany’s invasion of&nbsp;
Poitou to claim the province for himself. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:10.880
late July, Eleanor set out from Fontevrault with a&nbsp;
military escort to install herself at Poitiers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.880 --> 00:49:17.280
deter Arthur’s advance. She stopped on the way to&nbsp;
lodge at the castle of Mirabeau some twenty miles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.280 --> 00:49:23.600
north-east of Poitiers. However, Arthur learned&nbsp;
of her whereabouts, and then besieged the castle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:23.600 --> 00:49:29.360
intending to take the old queen hostage.&nbsp;
Instructing her men to defend the fortress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:29.360 --> 00:49:34.880
Eleanor smuggled out a message to John,&nbsp;
requesting aid. Upon receiving the letter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:41.600
the King immediately marched to the castle,&nbsp;
arriving on August 1st, and in a surprise attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:41.600 --> 00:49:49.280
the besiegers were overcome, Arthur captured, and&nbsp;
Eleanor freed and escorted to safety. In her final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:49.280 --> 00:49:56.160
defining act, Eleanor had helped John to achieve&nbsp;
the greatest military victory of his reign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:56.160 --> 00:50:02.400
Returning to Fontevrault Eleanor took the veil and&nbsp;
became a nun for the remainder of her life. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:02.400 --> 00:50:13.440
died on the 1st of April 1204 at the age of 82 and&nbsp;
was buried in between her husband and her son. 
&nbsp;

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:20.480
During her final years, the old Angevin empire was&nbsp;
falling apart around her as the ineffectiveness of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:20.480 --> 00:50:28.480
King John was ruthlessly exploited by Philip of&nbsp;
France. By 1205 he had taken control of Normandy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.480 --> 00:50:34.320
Anjou and Poitou. Eleanor’s immediate legacy&nbsp;
was marred, by the fall of her empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:39.840
and medieval thirteenth-century chroniclers&nbsp;
ensured her reputation was dominated by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:39.840 --> 00:50:45.840
rumours of the scandals of her youth, rather&nbsp;
than the wise and pious rule that defined her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:53.200
later years. In reality, the collapse of John’s&nbsp;
continental holdings following Eleanor’s death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:53.200 --> 00:50:59.360
demonstrates her skill in managing the political&nbsp;
and military in France while she was alive. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:59.360 --> 00:51:04.880
was only when Eleanor withdrew from political&nbsp;
life that the empire she had spent so much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:04.880 --> 00:51:11.040
her life working to uphold collapsed. The fact&nbsp;
that the life of Eleanor of Aquitaine has been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:17.920
and continues to be, surrounded by legend and&nbsp;
myth, speaks to the fierce grip she held on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:17.920 --> 00:51:24.480
imagination of medieval Europe. She has gone from&nbsp;
being portrayed as an evil and scandalous queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.480 --> 00:51:31.440
throughout the nineteenth century, to a romantic&nbsp;
heroine in the twentieth. Even today her failings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:31.440 --> 00:51:38.400
and her achievements are often exaggerated.&nbsp;
Eleanor had a remarkably long career as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:38.400 --> 00:51:44.880
political figure in Europe and an important&nbsp;
impact on history, in an era when women were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:51.360
so often relegated to a servile role. Indeed,&nbsp;
there are not many more influential figures in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:51.360 --> 00:51:57.920
the history of medieval Europe. As the nuns of&nbsp;
Fontevrault wrote after she died, “she surpassed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:57.920 --> 00:52:07.280
almost all the queens of the world.” 
What do you think of Eleanor of Aquitaine?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:07.280 --> 00:52:14.080
Was she really a charming seductress, using her&nbsp;
attractiveness to the greatest degree possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.080 --> 00:52:21.280
or was she an intelligent stateswoman primarily&nbsp;
motivated by fierce loyalty to her children?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:21.280 --> 00:52:29.440
Please let us know in the comment section, and in&nbsp;
the meantime, thank you very much for watching.

